-    "content": "# [Guide] Admin Diagnostics and Link Inspector Studio Guide and Architecture Walkthrough\n\nAuthoritative operational runbook and engineering architecture guide for the Unified Admin Diagnostics Studio, Link Inspector Studio, real-time subsystem telemetry, and Universal Object Graph defect remediation.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Executive Summary and Architecture Overview\n\nThe Unified Admin Diagnostics Studio (mounted at `/admin/diagnostics` and `/admin/link-auditor`) provides enterprise platform administrators with continuous visibility into system health, database migration integrity, Row-Level Security (RLS) enforcement, event relay queues, background worker execution, and object link topology across \ud83d\udc4dkanban.\n\nDesigned under Security Priority #0 and Agent-First Priority #1, the Diagnostics Studio serves two distinct operational functions:\n1. **Real-Time Subsystem Health Probing (`/admin/diagnostics`):** Collects synchronous and asynchronous telemetry across PostgreSQL connectivity, connection pool saturation, 76 single-head database migrations, PostgreSQL `FORCE RLS` policy status, transactional outbox message lag, WebSocket hub subscriptions, background job executor states, and Wasmtime/nsjail sandboxed execution runners.\n2. **Universal Object Graph Link Inspector & Purger (`/admin/link-auditor`):** Discovers, classifies, inspects, and remediates referential integrity anomalies across the Universal Object Graph without locking production tables or requiring downtime.\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    UI[\"Admin Diagnostics Studio UI (/admin/diagnostics, /admin/link-auditor)\"] --> AAL2[\"AAL2 Session Gate (require_admin_aal2)\"]\n    AAL2 --> Router[\"FastAPI Diagnostics & Link Auditor Router (/api/v1/admin/*)\"]\n    Router --> DBProbe[\"PostgreSQL Health & Migration Check (76 Migrations, 92 Tables)\"]\n    Router --> RLSProbe[\"PostgreSQL FORCE RLS Policy Audit\"]\n    Router --> RelayProbe[\"Outbox Relay Broker Telemetry (database/outbox)\"]\n    Router --> WSProbe[\"WebSocket Realtime Hub Ping/Pong Frame Telemetry\"]\n    Router --> WasmProbe[\"Wasmtime & nsjail Sandbox Worker Telemetry\"]\n    Router --> Auditor[\"Link Auditor Engine (6 Defect Classes)\"]\n    Auditor --> Inspector[\"Forensic Link Inspection Drawer\"]\n    Auditor --> Purge[\"Two-Stage Destructive Confirmation & Blast-Radius Engine\"]\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Multi-Tier Security, Authentication, and AAL2 Session Governance\n\nEvery endpoint mounted under `/api/v1/admin/diagnostics` and `/api/v1/admin/link-auditor` enforces Authenticator Assurance Level 2 (AAL2) multi-factor authentication. \n\n### Security Invariants\n- **AAL2 Session Dependency:** Route declarations strictly mandate `Depends(require_admin_aal2)` and `Depends(require_server_admin)` at both the router mount and endpoint handler levels.\n- **Fail-Closed Route Protection:** Automated tests (`assert_admin_routes_protected`) scan the OpenAPI routing table at build time. Any administrative route lacking explicit AAL2 session validation fails CI/CD immediately.\n- **Zero-Information Exposure:** Unauthenticated or unauthorized callers receive RFC 7807 problem responses without leaking database schema versions, internal hostnames, CIDR blocks, or subsystem topology.\n\n```python\n# Architecture contract in kanban/api/v1/admin/diagnostics.py\nrouter = APIRouter(\n    prefix=\"/admin/diagnostics\",\n    tags=[\"admin-diagnostics\"],\n    dependencies=[Depends(require_admin_aal2)],\n)\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Real-Time Telemetry and Subsystem Probes\n\nThe Diagnostics Studio executes comprehensive health checks against core application subsystems, reporting structured telemetry to the administrative UI and monitoring agents:\n\n### Telemetry Subsystem Breakdown\n\n| Subsystem Component | Metric & Probe Target | Healthy Threshold | Degraded Condition | Remediation Action |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **PostgreSQL Engine** | Latency, connection pool saturation (`pool_size`, `checked_out`) | `< 15ms`, saturation `< 75%` | Latency `> 50ms`, pool exhausted | Scale connection pool (`DB_POOL_SIZE`), tune PostgreSQL `max_connections` |\n| **Migration Consistency** | Current version vs single-head Alembic tree | `76 / 76` migrations applied | Head mismatch or split branches | Run `kanban-ctl db migrate` or verify Alembic head |\n| **Row-Level Security** | `FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY` status on all tables | 100% (92 of 92 tables) | Any table lacking `FORCE RLS` | Run `kanban-ctl db audit-rls` and apply missing security policies |\n| **Transactional Outbox** | Queue backlog, dispatch latency, relay loop status | Lag `< 5s`, relay `running` | Backlog `> 1000` events, relay stopped | Inspect `database/outbox/relay.py` worker process and restart daemon |\n| **Realtime WebSocket Hub** | Active client connections, ping/pong frame roundtrip | Ping latency `< 50ms` | Stalled connections, high dropped frames | Inspect Caddy/reverse-proxy timeouts and client network conditions |\n| **Async Job Executor** | Active tasks, dead letter queue (DLQ) depth | DLQ depth `= 0` | DLQ depth `> 0`, executor stalled | Inspect dead-letter payloads, clear retry budgets, check worker logs |\n| **Sandboxed Runners** | Wasmtime AST compiler and nsjail container isolation | Wasmtime initialized, nsjail active | nsjail unavailable (fallback to standalone) | Check kernel namespace support (`CONFIG_USER_NS`) and cgroup limits |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Universal Object Graph Link Auditor and Inspector Studio\n\n\ud83d\udc4dkanban models all work items, digital stickies, technical documents, sprint cycles, and modules as registered entities connected via typed `object_links`. The Link Auditor continuously detects and repairs referential anomalies.\n\n### The Six Defect Classes\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\n    class ObjectLinkDefect {\n        <<enumeration>>\n        ORPHAN_SOURCE\n        ORPHAN_TARGET\n        CROSS_WORKSPACE\n        INVALID_KIND\n        INVERTED_DEPENDENCY\n        DANGLING_PARENT\n    }\n```\n\n1. **`ORPHAN_SOURCE`:** The link record specifies a `source_id` entity that has been hard-deleted or does not exist in PostgreSQL.\n2. **`ORPHAN_TARGET`:** The link record specifies a `target_id` entity that no longer exists in PostgreSQL.\n3. **`CROSS_WORKSPACE`:** The link connects two entities across distinct workspace tenant boundaries without an active federation agreement, violating multi-tenant isolation.\n4. **`INVALID_KIND`:** The link record specifies an unregistered or deprecated relation kind (valid kinds: `BLOCKS`, `BLOCKED_BY`, `RELATES_TO`, `DUPLICATE_OF`, `PARENT_OF`, `CHILD_OF`).\n5. **`INVERTED_DEPENDENCY`:** An illegal cyclic or inverted dependency violates topological sort order (e.g. Issue A blocks Issue B while Issue B blocks Issue A).\n6. **`DANGLING_PARENT`:** A hierarchical child item references a parent container that has been archived, shredded, or purged.\n\n### Forensic Link Inspection Drawer\n\nThe Link Auditor interface renders defective links in an interactive inspection drawer. Administrators can review:\n- Source and target entity types (`issue`, `sticky`, `doc`, `cycle`, `module`).\n- Link kind and timestamp of creation.\n- Root cause forensic classification.\n- Impacted workspace and project keys.\n\n### Two-Stage Destructive Confirmation and Blast-Radius Engine\n\nTo prevent accidental data loss during remediation, link purging requires explicit two-stage confirmation:\n1. **Stage 1 (Blast-Radius Calculation):** The administrator requests a purge dry run (`POST /api/v1/admin/link-auditor/purge?dry_run=true`). The server computes the exact list of link IDs scheduled for removal and records a cryptographic summary in session state.\n2. **Stage 2 (Cryptographic Confirmation):** The administrator confirms the operation by providing the exact confirmation token generated in Stage 1. The server deletes the invalid links inside a single transactional block and writes an immutable entry to the SHA-256 hash-chained `audit_events` ledger.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"operation\": \"PURGE_INVALID_OBJECT_LINKS\",\n  \"purged_count\": 14,\n  \"defect_breakdown\": {\n    \"ORPHAN_TARGET\": 9,\n    \"CROSS_WORKSPACE\": 3,\n    \"INVALID_KIND\": 2\n  },\n  \"dry_run\": false,\n  \"confirmed_by_principal\": \"usr_admin_01\",\n  \"audit_event_id\": \"aud_018f92b7c4d173e2\"\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Operational Runbook: Inspections and Disaster Recovery\n\n### Routine Diagnostic Inspection\n\n1. Navigate to `/admin/diagnostics` in the web application using an account with `SERVER_ADMIN` credentials.\n2. Verify that all status indicators in the Subsystem Overview grid display **Operational** (`green`).\n3. Click **Run Health Probe** to execute active roundtrip latency measurements against the database, outbox relay, and WebSocket hub.\n4. Review the **Migration State** card to confirm 76/76 migrations are applied with single-head consistency.\n5. If any subsystem reports **Degraded** or **Down**, click **Inspect Subsystem** to view raw exception logs and performance percentiles.\n\n### Link Graph Remediation Procedure\n\n1. Switch to the **Link Inspector** tab or navigate directly to `/admin/link-auditor`.\n2. Click **Start Comprehensive Scan** (`GET /api/v1/admin/link-auditor/scan`).\n3. Review the defect summary cards grouped by defect class (`ORPHAN_SOURCE`, `ORPHAN_TARGET`, `CROSS_WORKSPACE`, etc.).\n4. Click on any row to open the **Forensic Inspection Drawer** and review the link metadata.\n5. Click **Purge Invalid Links** to open the remediation modal.\n6. Review the calculated blast radius. Type the confirmation phrase `PURGE INVALID LINKS` and confirm.\n7. Verify that the operation completes successfully and inspect the updated link audit counter (expected: 0 defects).\n\n### Command-Line Diagnostics (Headless & Air-Gapped Environments)\n\nFor headless container environments or automated CI/CD maintenance jobs, platform administrators can invoke equivalent diagnostics via `kanban-ctl`:\n\n```bash\n# Verify database connection and single-head migration consistency\nkanban-ctl db check-migrations\n\n# Audit all 92 tables for Row-Level Security enforcement\nkanban-ctl db audit-rls\n\n# Scan universal object graph for orphaned or corrupt links\nkanban-ctl db audit-links --verbose\n\n# Perform dry-run link purge\nkanban-ctl db purge-invalid-links --dry-run\n\n# Execute audited link purge and write to audit chain\nkanban-ctl db purge-invalid-links --confirm\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. Visual Reference and Studio Screenshots\n\nThe Admin Diagnostics Studio provides a clean, breathable interface matching the \ud83d\udc4dkanban design system tokens:\n\n![Admin Link Auditor Overview](images/46-admin-link-auditor.png)\n\n![Link Auditor Forensic Inspection Drawer](images/47-admin-link-auditor-inspect-drawer.png)\n\n![Link Auditor Purge Blast-Radius Modal](images/48-admin-link-auditor-purge-modal.png)\n\n![Admin Browser QA and Diagnostics View](images/68-admin-browser-qa.png)\n"
+    "content": "# [Guide] Admin Diagnostics and Link Inspector Studio Guide and Architecture Walkthrough\n\nAuthoritative operational runbook and engineering architecture guide for the Unified Admin Diagnostics Studio, Link Inspector Studio, real-time subsystem telemetry, and Universal Object Graph defect remediation.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Executive Summary and Architecture Overview\n\nThe Unified Admin Diagnostics Studio (mounted at `/admin/diagnostics` and `/admin/link-auditor`) provides enterprise platform administrators with continuous visibility into system health, database migration integrity, Row-Level Security (RLS) enforcement, event relay queues, background worker execution, and object link topology across \ud83d\udc4dkanban.\n\nDesigned under Security Priority #0 and Agent-First Priority #1, the Diagnostics Studio serves two distinct operational functions:\n1. **Real-Time Subsystem Health Probing (`/admin/diagnostics`):** Collects synchronous and asynchronous telemetry across 7 core subsystems: PostgreSQL RLS connectivity, transactional outbox message lag, background job worker pools, Wasmtime sandboxed execution runners, AI provider runtime availability, remote mTLS runner nodes, and request rate limiters.\n2. **Universal Object Graph Link Inspector & Purger (`/admin/link-auditor`):** Discovers, classifies, inspects, and remediates referential integrity anomalies across 7 invalidity classes without locking production tables or requiring system downtime.\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    UI[\"Admin Diagnostics Studio UI (/admin/diagnostics, /admin/link-auditor)\"] --> AAL2[\"AAL2 Session Gate (require_admin_aal2)\"]\n    AAL2 --> Router[\"FastAPI Diagnostics & Link Auditor Router (/api/v1/admin/*)\"]\n    Router --> DBProbe[\"PostgreSQL Health & Migration Check (82 Migrations, 100 Tables)\"]\n    Router --> RLSProbe[\"PostgreSQL FORCE RLS Policy Audit\"]\n    Router --> RelayProbe[\"Outbox Relay Broker Telemetry (database/outbox)\"]\n    Router --> WSProbe[\"WebSocket Realtime Hub Ping/Pong Frame Telemetry\"]\n    Router --> WasmProbe[\"Wasmtime & nsjail Sandbox Worker Telemetry\"]\n    Router --> Auditor[\"Link Auditor Engine (7 Defect Classes)\"]\n    Auditor --> Inspector[\"Forensic Link Inspection Drawer\"]\n    Auditor --> Purge[\"Two-Stage Destructive Confirmation & Blast-Radius Engine\"]\n```\n\n![Admin Diagnostics Studio - Desktop Light](images/slice4-diagnostics-1440x900-light.png)\n\n---\n\n## 2. Multi-Tier Security, Authentication, and AAL2 Session Governance\n\nEvery endpoint mounted under `/api/v1/admin/diagnostics` and `/api/v1/admin/object-links:*` enforces Authenticator Assurance Level 2 (AAL2) multi-factor authentication. \n\n### Security Invariants\n- **AAL2 Session Dependency:** Route declarations strictly mandate `Depends(require_admin_aal2)` and `Depends(require_server_admin)` at both the router mount and endpoint handler levels.\n- **Fail-Closed Route Protection:** Automated test suites (`assert_admin_routes_protected`) scan the OpenAPI routing table at build time. Any administrative route lacking explicit AAL2 session validation fails CI/CD immediately.\n- **Zero-Information Exposure:** Unauthenticated callers receive RFC 7807 problem responses without leaking database schema versions, internal hostnames, CIDR blocks, or subsystem topology.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Real-Time Telemetry and the 7 Core Subsystems\n\nThe Diagnostics Studio executes comprehensive health checks against core application subsystems, reporting structured telemetry to the administrative UI and monitoring agents:\n\n### Subsystem Telemetry Breakdown\n\n| Subsystem Component | Metric & Probe Target | Healthy Threshold | Degraded Condition | Remediation Action |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **1. PostgreSQL Engine (`postgresql_rls`)** | Latency, connection pool saturation, 82 single-head migrations, `FORCE RLS` on 100 tables | Latency < 15ms, saturation < 75%, 82/82 migrations | Latency > 50ms, pool exhausted, missing migration | Tune `DB_POOL_SIZE`, check Alembic single head, apply missing migrations |\n| **2. Transactional Outbox (`realtime_hub_outbox`)** | Queue backlog, dispatch latency, relay loop status, PostgreSQL LISTEN/NOTIFY | Lag < 5s, relay running | Backlog > 1000 events, relay stopped | Inspect `database/outbox/relay.py` worker process and restart daemon |\n| **3. Job Worker Pool (`job_worker_pool`)** | Active background tasks, concurrency limits, dead-letter queue (DLQ) depth | DLQ depth = 0 | DLQ depth > 0, executor stalled | Inspect failed task payloads, verify resource limits, restart worker |\n| **4. Wasmtime Sandbox (`wasm_script_sandbox`)** | Wasmtime compiler initialization, memory consumption, execution fuel budget | Memory < 64MB, fuel active | Wasmtime compilation failure, fuel exhausted | Inspect custom field scripts for infinite loops, allocate fuel |\n| **5. AI Agent Runtime (`agent_runtime_providers`)** | Provider availability (Ollama, OpenAI, Gemini), response latency, monthly token budget | Primary provider healthy, budget < 90% | Provider timeout, token cap exceeded | Rotate fallback provider, adjust monthly token ceiling |\n| **6. Remote Runner Fleet (`remote_runner_fleet`)** | Connected build nodes over port 8444, mTLS heartbeat status, active task leases | >= 1 runner online, heartbeat < 30s | All runners offline, expired CSR tokens | Mint fresh enrollment token, verify network port :8444 ingress |\n| **7. API Rate Limiter (`rate_limiter`)** | In-memory token bucket capacity, blocked requests, IP throttle health | System error rate = 0% | Memory leak in bucket map, false-positive blocks | Clear expired IP throttle buckets, adjust tier limits |\n\n![Admin Diagnostics Realtime Probe Active](images/slice4-diagnostics-realtime-probe-active.png)\n\n### Real-Time Diagnostics Probes\n\nThe studio provides two active latency probes for testing live message paths:\n\n1. **PostgreSQL Pub/Sub Probe (`POST /api/v1/admin/diagnostics/probes/realtime`):**\n   Issues a synthetic transactional outbox message and measures the exact roundtrip latency for PostgreSQL `LISTEN` and `NOTIFY` notification delivery. Healthy roundtrips execute in under 1.0 ms.\n\n2. **WebSocket Realtime Probe (`/ws` ping frame):**\n   Initiates a live WebSocket connection from the browser client to the server's `/ws` endpoint, transmits a binary ping frame, and captures the roundtrip pong latency. Live connections establish in ~13 ms with roundtrip latency < 1 ms.\n\n![Admin Diagnostics Studio - Desktop Dark](images/slice4-diagnostics-1440x900-dark.png)\n\n---\n\n## 4. Universal Object Graph Link Auditor Studio (`/admin/link-auditor`)\n\n\ud83d\udc4dkanban models all work items, digital stickies, technical documents, sprint cycles, and modules as registered entities connected via typed `object_links`. The Link Auditor continuously detects and repairs referential anomalies.\n\n![Link Auditor Studio - Desktop Light](images/slice4-link-auditor-1440x900-light.png)\n\n### The 7 Invalidity Classes\n\nThe Link Auditor (`POST /api/v1/admin/object-links:audit`) evaluates every link against 7 formal invalidity classes:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    Scan[\"Integrity Audit Scan (POST /api/v1/admin/object-links:audit)\"] --> C1[\"1. DANGLING_TARGET<br/>Target object UUID missing from PostgreSQL\"]\n    Scan --> C2[\"2. DANGLING_SOURCE<br/>Source object UUID missing from PostgreSQL\"]\n    Scan --> C3[\"3. EXPIRED_ENDPOINT<br/>Endpoint object soft-deleted past retention window\"]\n    Scan --> C4[\"4. TENANT_MISMATCH<br/>Source and target in disjoint tenants without federation\"]\n    Scan --> C5[\"5. TYPE_UNREGISTERED<br/>link_type not registered in system schema registry\"]\n    Scan --> C6[\"6. CARDINALITY_VIOLATION<br/>Violates registered 1:1 or 1:N link cardinality\"]\n    Scan --> C7[\"7. SELF_REFERENTIAL_CYCLE<br/>Forbidden cycle in directed acyclic graph hierarchy\"]\n```\n\n1. **`DANGLING_TARGET`:** The link record specifies a `target_id` entity that has been hard-deleted or does not exist in the database.\n2. **`DANGLING_SOURCE`:** The link record specifies a `source_id` entity that no longer exists in the database.\n3. **`EXPIRED_ENDPOINT`:** One or both endpoints of the link reference an object soft-deleted beyond the workspace retention threshold.\n4. **`TENANT_MISMATCH`:** The link connects two entities across distinct workspace tenant boundaries without an active federation agreement, violating multi-tenant isolation.\n5. **`TYPE_UNREGISTERED`:** The link record specifies an unregistered or deprecated relation kind (valid kinds: `BLOCKS`, `BLOCKED_BY`, `RELATES_TO`, `DUPLICATE_OF`, `PARENT_OF`, `CHILD_OF`).\n6. **`CARDINALITY_VIOLATION`:** The relation violates registered cardinality constraints (e.g. an issue having multiple distinct parent containers).\n7. **`SELF_REFERENTIAL_CYCLE`:** An illegal circular dependency violates directed acyclic graph invariants (e.g. Issue A blocks Issue B while Issue B blocks Issue A).\n\n![Link Auditor Integrity Scan Active](images/slice4-link-auditor-integrity-scan-active.png)\n\n### Two-Stage Audited Purge & Mandatory Justification\n\nTo prevent accidental data loss during graph remediation, purging invalid links requires two-stage execution and audited justification:\n\n1. **Mandatory Justification (`>= 10` characters):**\n   The administrator must supply a written rationale explaining the purge operation (e.g. `\"Decommissioning legacy test links post-cutover\"`). Rationale strings shorter than 10 characters are strictly rejected with HTTP 422 before any deletion occurs.\n\n2. **Immutable Audit Ledger Recording:**\n   When the purge executes (`POST /api/v1/admin/object-links:purge`), the deleted links are removed in a single atomic transaction. The server records an immutable `INVALID_OBJECT_LINK_PURGED` event in the hash-chained `activity_records` ledger, logging the purged link IDs, defect classes, operator identity, and justification string.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"operation\": \"PURGE_INVALID_OBJECT_LINKS\",\n  \"purged_count\": 2,\n  \"defect_breakdown\": {\n    \"DANGLING_TARGET\": 1,\n    \"EXPIRED_ENDPOINT\": 1\n  },\n  \"justification\": \"Purging orphaned test links following Slice-4 cutover verification\",\n  \"confirmed_by_principal\": \"adamoutler@gmail.com\",\n  \"audit_event_id\": \"aud_01a21394b8e27c1a\"\n}\n```\n\n![Link Auditor Studio - Desktop Dark](images/slice4-link-auditor-1440x900-dark.png)\n\n---\n\n## 5. Operational Runbooks: Inspections and Remediation\n\n### Runbook 1: Performing Routine Diagnostics and Realtime Health Probing\n\n1. In the web interface, navigate to `/admin/diagnostics` using an account with `SERVER_ADMIN` privileges.\n2. Complete the WebSudo AAL2 step-up challenge if prompted.\n3. Inspect the Subsystem Health Grid. Verify that all 7 subsystems display **Operational** (`green`).\n4. Click **Run Health Probe**. The system issues active PostgreSQL outbox and WebSocket ping probes. Verify that database query latency is < 15ms and WebSocket RTT is < 50ms.\n5. Inspect the **Runtime and Container** card to verify non-root UID execution, cgroup memory limits, and container runtime details.\n\n![Admin Diagnostics - Tablet Landscape](images/slice4-diagnostics-1024x768-tablet-landscape.png)\n\n### Runbook 2: Scanning and Purging Invalid Object Links\n\n1. Switch to the **Link Inspector** tab or navigate directly to `/admin/link-auditor`.\n2. Click **Start Comprehensive Scan** (`POST /api/v1/admin/object-links:audit`).\n3. Review the defect summary cards grouped by the 7 defect classes.\n4. If defects are identified, click on any row to open the **Forensic Inspection Drawer** and review the link metadata, source/target IDs, and timestamps.\n5. Click **Purge Invalid Links** to open the remediation dialog.\n6. Enter an operational justification of at least 10 characters describing why these links are being purged.\n7. Click **Confirm Purge**. The backend executes the audited deletion and writes the audit ledger entry.\n8. Re-run the scan to confirm zero defects remain.\n\n![Link Auditor - Mobile Portrait](images/slice4-link-auditor-390x844-mobile-portrait.png)\n"
