-    "content": "# [Guide] Production Promotion and Backup Runbook\n\n## Overview\n\nTeams frequently evaluate software in a standalone environment before promoting it to a production cluster. In conventional ticketing platforms, moving from a single test container to a hardened multi-container architecture requires painful manual database migrations, re-uploading media assets, and rebuilding configuration.\n\n\ud83d\udc4dkanban provides a streamlined, reliable **Production Promotion and Encrypted Backup Architecture** (`docs/PROMOTION_AND_BACKUP.md`). With standardized tools, administrators can promote standalone deployments or execute disaster recovery with minimal downtime.\n\n---\n\n## Standalone to Production Promotion Workflow\n\nMoving from a single-container standalone instance (`docker run`) to the 5-network production stack (`docker compose`) takes five sequential steps:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    S1[\"Step 1: Drain Active Traffic<br/>kanban-ctl promote --drain --timeout=30s<br/>Halt requests and flush WAL\"]\n    S2[\"Step 2: Export Production Compose Bundle<br/>kanban-ctl promote --export-compose ./deploy/prod/<br/>Generate 5-network compose and config\"]\n    S3[\"Step 3: Transfer PostgreSQL Data Volume<br/>Copy /var/lib/kanban/data to production volume<br/>Hardware-level data preservation\"]\n    S4[\"Step 4: Launch Production Stack<br/>docker compose -f deploy/prod/docker-compose.yml up -d<br/>Compartmentalized 5-network isolation\"]\n    S5[\"Step 5: Post-Promotion Verification<br/>kanban-ctl health --full && kanban-ctl audit-links<br/>Verify 100% integrity\"]\n\n    S1 --> S2\n    S2 --> S3\n    S3 --> S4\n    S4 --> S5\n```\n\n![Standalone to Production Compose Export Modal](images/27-standalone-compose-export.png)\n\n### Detailed Execution Steps\n\n#### Step 1: Drain Active Traffic\nHalt incoming requests and flush PostgreSQL Write-Ahead Logs (WAL):\n```bash\ndocker exec -it thumbs-up-kanban kanban-ctl promote --drain --timeout=30s\n```\n\n#### Step 2: Export Production Configuration\nGenerate the hardened 5-network `docker-compose.yml` and environment templates from the running instance:\n```bash\ndocker exec -it thumbs-up-kanban kanban-ctl promote --export-compose /tmp/deploy/\ndocker cp thumbs-up-kanban:/tmp/deploy ./deploy-prod\n```\n\n#### Step 3: Transfer Persistent Volumes\nCopy the internal database directory to the dedicated production storage volume:\n```bash\ndocker volume create kanban_postgres_prod_data\ndocker run --rm \\\n  -v kanban_data:/from:ro \\\n  -v kanban_postgres_prod_data:/to \\\n  alpine cp -a /from/. /to/\n```\n\n#### Step 4: Launch Production Stack\nStart the compartmentalized multi-container cluster:\n```bash\ncd deploy-prod\ndocker compose up -d\n```\n\n#### Step 5: Post-Promotion Verification\nConfirm service availability and object graph integrity:\n```bash\ndocker compose exec app kanban-ctl health --full\ndocker compose exec app kanban-ctl audit-links --dry-run\n```\n\n---\n\n## Encrypted KBBAK1 Backup Archive Format\n\n\ud83d\udc4dkanban implements a unified backup format named **KBBAK1**. Rather than taking unencrypted raw SQL dumps, KBBAK1 produces an encrypted, tamper-evident bundle:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    subgraph Contents[\"KBBAK1 Archive Members\"]\n        M[\"manifest.json<br/>(SHA-256 Checksums, Versions)\"]\n        D[\"database.sql.gz<br/>(Complete PostgreSQL Dump)\"]\n        O[\"object_graph.jsonl<br/>(Universal Spine & Links)\"]\n        A[\"media/<br/>(Attachments & Visual Assets)\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph Crypto[\"Cryptographic Envelope\"]\n        KDF[\"PBKDF2 KDF<br/>(600,000 Iterations)\"]\n        AES[\"AES-256-GCM<br/>Ciphertext Stream\"]\n    end\n\n    Contents --> KDF\n    KDF --> AES\n    AES --> Bundle[\".kbbak1 Encrypted Bundle\"]\n```\n\n### KBBAK1 Archive Structure\n- **`manifest.json`:** Contains platform version, schema migration hash, tenant IDs, and SHA-256 checksums of all internal components.\n- **`database.sql.gz`:** Complete PostgreSQL cluster dump including all schemas, RLS policies, and extensions.\n- **`object_graph.jsonl`:** Portable Universal Object Graph spine and typed links export for cross-version reconciliation.\n- **`media/`:** Uploaded attachments, document assets, and user avatars.\n\n### Cryptographic Envelope\nThe entire tarball is encrypted using AES-256-GCM. The encryption key is derived from the administrative backup secret (`BACKUP_KEY`) using PBKDF2 with 600,000 iterations:\n```bash\n# Create an encrypted backup archive\nkanban-ctl backup create \\\n  --encrypt \\\n  --output /var/backups/kanban-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S).kbbak1\n```\n\n![System Settings and Environment Configuration](images/26-system-settings.png)\n\n---\n\n## Visual Backup and Disaster Recovery Studio (`/admin/standalone`)\n\nIn addition to CLI automation, administrators managing standalone and hybrid deployments can trigger backups, configure schedules, and execute disaster recovery through the visual studio at `/admin/standalone`.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    subgraph StandaloneStudio[\"System Storage, Backups & Deployment Studio\"]\n        Tab1[\"Storage & Capabilities Health<br/>Disk usage, DB size, WAL status\"]\n        Tab2[\"Backup, Auto-Backup & Restore<br/>Manual Encrypted (.kbbak), Schedules, 2-Stage Restore\"]\n        Tab3[\"Docker Compose Export<br/>Hardened multi-container production bundle\"]\n    end\n```\n\n![Visual Backup and Disaster Recovery Studio](images/57-standalone-backup-restore-modal.png)\n\n### Studio Features:\n1. **Manual Encrypted Backup (`.kbbak`):** Enter a client-side passphrase (minimum 16 characters) to generate and download an AES-256-GCM encrypted snapshot directly to the operator's machine. The passphrase is never stored on the server.\n2. **Automated Scheduled Backups:** Enable periodic automated backups with customizable backup intervals (e.g. every 24 hours) and snapshot retention windows (e.g. keep latest 7 snapshots).\n3. **Existing Server Snapshots List:** Browse, inspect file sizes, creation timestamps, and download historical server snapshots with a single click.\n4. **Two-Stage Database Restore:** Restoring a database snapshot requires two-stage verification: Step 1 verifies cryptographic archive integrity without touching the database; Step 2 requires explicit typed confirmation before applying the restore.\n\n---\n\n## Disaster Recovery and Restoration\n\nRestoring a cluster from an encrypted backup archive is completely automated.\n\n```bash\n# Step 1: Validate archive integrity without restoring\nkanban-ctl backup verify --input /var/backups/kanban-snapshot.kbbak1\n\n# Step 2: Execute full disaster recovery restoration\nkanban-ctl backup restore \\\n  --input /var/backups/kanban-snapshot.kbbak1 \\\n  --decrypt \\\n  --confirm-overwrite\n```\n\n### Post-Restore Automated Verification\nFollowing database restoration:\n1. All database sequences are automatically synchronized.\n2. The **Invalid Object Link Auditor** executes immediately to verify referential integrity.\n3. RLS policies are audited to confirm that `kanban_app` lacks bypass permissions.\n4. Active WebSocket sessions are broadcast a reconnect signal.\n"
+    "content": "# [Guide] Production Promotion and Encrypted Backup Runbook\n\n## Overview\n\nTeams frequently evaluate software in a lightweight standalone environment before promoting it to a hardened production cluster. In conventional ticketing platforms, moving from a single test container to a production architecture requires painful manual database migrations, re-uploading media assets, and rebuilding configurations from scratch.\n\n\ud83d\udc4dkanban provides a streamlined, reliable **Production Promotion and Encrypted Backup Architecture** (`docs/PROMOTION_AND_BACKUP.md`). With standardized tools and privilege-separated daemons, administrators can promote standalone deployments or execute disaster recovery with minimal downtime and cryptographic certainty.\n\n---\n\n## Standalone to Production Promotion Workflow\n\nMoving from a single-container standalone instance (`docker run -d -p 8080:8080 thumbs-up-kanban:latest`) to the compartmentalized 5-network production stack (`docker compose`) takes five sequential steps:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    S1[\"Step 1: Drain Active Traffic<br/>kanban-ctl promote --drain --timeout=30s<br/>Halt requests and flush WAL\"]\n    S2[\"Step 2: Export Production Compose Bundle<br/>GET /api/v1/admin/standalone/compose-export<br/>Generate 5-network compose and scrubbed .env\"]\n    S3[\"Step 3: Transfer PostgreSQL Data Volume<br/>Copy /var/lib/kanban/data to production volume<br/>Hardware-level data preservation\"]\n    S4[\"Step 4: Launch Production Stack<br/>docker compose -f deploy/prod/docker-compose.yml up -d<br/>Compartmentalized 5-network isolation\"]\n    S5[\"Step 5: Post-Promotion Verification<br/>kanban-ctl health --full && kanban-ctl audit-links<br/>Verify 100% integrity\"]\n\n    S1 --> S2\n    S2 --> S3\n    S3 --> S4\n    S4 --> S5\n```\n\n![Standalone Status and Backups Studio - Desktop Light](images/slice4-standalone-1440x900-light.png)\n\n### The 5 Isolated Production Networks\n\nThe generated production `docker-compose.yml` divides services across five strictly isolated Docker bridge networks:\n1. **`edge_net`:** Connects the Caddy TLS ingress proxy to the external network and routes HTTPS traffic into the web/API service. No database or Redis containers attach to `edge_net`.\n2. **`app_net`:** Connects the FastAPI backend service to the web client, internal caches, and the outbox event relay.\n3. **`data_net`:** Restricted network connecting the backend service strictly to PostgreSQL 16 and Redis. Containers on `edge_net` or `sandbox_net` have zero network route to `data_net`.\n4. **`sandbox_net`:** Air-gapped network for untrusted custom field scripts and Wasmtime runners, blocking all external egress.\n5. **`egress_llm_net`:** Egress-only network allowing AI provider connections (OpenAI, Gemini, Ollama) and mTLS runner heartbeats on port 8444 while preventing inbound lateral movement.\n\n---\n\n## Encrypted Backup Formats: KBKP Streaming vs KBBAK1 Snapshots\n\n\ud83d\udc4dkanban implements dual encrypted backup engines engineered for distinct operational scenarios:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    subgraph DirectStream[\"Direct Download (KBKP Streaming)\"]\n        K1[\"POST /api/v1/admin/standalone/backup<br/>Streaming AES-256-GCM Envelope\"] --> K2[\"Direct Client Browser Download<br/>kanban-backup.kbbak (KBKP header)\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph ServerSnapshot[\"Automated Snapshots (KBBAK1 Archive)\"]\n        B1[\"POST /api/v1/admin/standalone/backups/run-now<br/>Fixed-op daemon (.kb_ops.sock)\"] --> B2[\"Stored in /var/lib/kanban/backups<br/>kanban-backup-TIMESTAMP.kbbak (KBBAK1)\"]\n    end\n```\n\n### 1. Streaming Direct Download (`KBKP` Format)\n- **Endpoint:** `POST /api/v1/admin/standalone/backup`\n- **Mechanism:** Streams an AES-256-GCM encrypted payload directly to the operator's web browser without buffering the full archive on server disk.\n- **Envelope Header:** Prefixed with 4-byte magic bytes `KBKP`, followed by a 16-byte cryptographic salt, a 12-byte GCM initialization vector (IV), and the ciphertext stream terminated with a 16-byte authentication tag.\n- **Passphrase Contract:** Derived via PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 with 600,000 iterations. Passphrases must be at least 16 characters in length. The passphrase exists ephemerally in memory and is actively scrubbed via `zeroize`.\n\n### 2. Server-Side Automated Snapshots (`KBBAK1` Format)\n- **Endpoint:** `POST /api/v1/admin/standalone/backups/run-now`\n- **Daemon Privilege Separation:** Managed by a dedicated, root-privileged local daemon communicating strictly over UNIX domain socket `/var/lib/kanban/.kb_ops.sock`. The web application process running as non-root user `kanban_app` cannot directly invoke shell scripts or bypass database controls; it issues fixed-operation IPC commands to `.kb_ops.sock`.\n- **Storage Location:** Saved in `/var/lib/kanban/backups` with ISO-8601 timestamps (e.g. `kanban-backup-20261125T144207Z.kbbak`).\n- **Archive Structure:** Tar archive prefixed with magic header `KBBAK1`, bundling `manifest.json` (SHA-256 component hashes), `database.sql.gz` (complete PostgreSQL dump), `object_graph.jsonl` (universal spine and links), and `media/` (binary assets).\n\n![Standalone Backups and Snapshot History](images/slice4-standalone-backups-snapshot-history.png)\n\n---\n\n## Visual Backup & Promotion Studio (`/admin/standalone`)\n\nThe Standalone Status and Backups Studio is mounted at `/admin/standalone` (and through the Administration navigation under Deployment & Backups). It provides three tabs:\n\n1. **Storage & Capabilities Health:** Inspects runtime deployment mode, disk mount points, total database size, WAL generation rate, and container cgroup memory/CPU limits.\n2. **Backups, Auto-Backup & Restore:** Triggers manual encrypted backups, schedules automated recurring snapshots, inspects historical server backups, and manages 2-stage restores.\n3. **Docker Compose Export:** Generates the production 5-network `docker-compose.yml` and secret-scrubbed `.env.example` file.\n\n![Standalone Status Studio - Desktop Dark](images/slice4-standalone-1440x900-dark.png)\n\n### Studio Controls & Backend Setting Reference\n\n1. **Passphrase Input (`passphrase`):**\n   Client-supplied encryption passphrase (minimum 16 characters). Used for client-side key derivation on direct downloads and for unlocking backup archives during restore. Never stored in PostgreSQL or logged.\n\n2. **Automated Backup Schedule (`enabled`, `interval_hours`):**\n   Configures periodic automated snapshots. Options for `interval_hours` include `6`, `12`, `24`, and `48` hours. Snapshots are created by the `.kb_ops.sock` background daemon.\n\n3. **Retention Policy (`retention_count`):**\n   Controls the number of automated snapshot files retained on disk (options range from `3` to `30`, default `7`). The snapshot purger automatically deletes older archives while preserving the newest snapshots.\n\n4. **Confirmation Overwrite (`confirm_overwrite`):**\n   Mandatory safety string required for destructive database restores (`POST /api/v1/admin/standalone/restore`). Must be set to the exact string `\"RESTORE\"`. Submissions with missing or incorrect confirmation strings are rejected with HTTP 422 before any database teardown begins.\n\n5. **Tamper Verification Gate:**\n   Prior to applying any restore, the backend executes `POST /api/v1/admin/standalone/backup/verify`. If even a single bit of ciphertext has been modified or corrupted, the GCM authentication tag verification fails and the operation halts with HTTP 422.\n\n![Standalone Status Studio - Tablet Landscape](images/slice4-standalone-1024x768-tablet-landscape.png)\n\n---\n\n## Step-by-Step Operator Runbooks\n\n### Runbook 1: Creating an Encrypted Backup Snapshot\n\n1. Navigate to `/admin/standalone` and open the **Backups** tab.\n2. Under **Manual Encrypted Backup**, enter an encryption passphrase meeting the 16-character minimum requirement.\n3. Click **Download Backup (.kbbak)**. The server streams the encrypted `KBKP` archive directly to your browser.\n4. Alternatively, click **Create Server Snapshot Now** to invoke the background daemon. The new snapshot appears in the Server Snapshots table with its exact byte size and timestamp.\n\n### Runbook 2: Restoring from an Encrypted Backup Archive\n\nRestoring a database snapshot completely replaces existing database state with the archive contents. Follow this two-stage procedure:\n\n1. In `/admin/standalone`, scroll to **Restore Database Snapshot**.\n2. Select an existing server snapshot from the dropdown, or upload a local `.kbbak` archive file.\n3. Enter the exact passphrase used when creating the backup.\n4. Click **Verify Archive Integrity**. The backend validates the GCM authentication tag and verifies internal component checksums without touching the database.\n5. In the confirmation input box, type the uppercase confirmation phrase `RESTORE`.\n6. Click **Execute Destructive Restore**. The backend enters single-user maintenance mode, terminates existing sessions, restores all tables and sequences, re-establishes RLS policies, and reconnects the application.\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant Admin as Administrator UI\n    participant API as FastAPI Backend (/api/v1/admin/standalone/*)\n    participant Ops as Ops Daemon (/var/lib/kanban/.kb_ops.sock)\n    participant DB as PostgreSQL 16 (kanban)\n\n    Admin->>API: POST /restore (archive, passphrase, confirm_overwrite=\"RESTORE\")\n    API->>API: Validate confirm_overwrite == \"RESTORE\" (422 if mismatched)\n    API->>API: Derive AES-256 key via PBKDF2 (600,000 rounds)\n    API->>API: Verify AES-256-GCM auth tag (422 if tampered/wrong passphrase)\n    API->>Ops: IPC RESTORE_SNAPSHOT(temp_archive_path)\n    Ops->>DB: Terminate connections & drop/re-create tables\n    Ops->>DB: Ingest database.sql.gz & restore sequences\n    Ops->>DB: Execute audit-rls (assert FORCE RLS on all tables)\n    Ops-->>API: Restore completed (100 tables restored)\n    API-->>Admin: HTTP 200 OK (Restore Successful)\n```\n\n### Runbook 3: Promoting Standalone to Production Multi-Container Compose\n\n1. In `/admin/standalone`, switch to the **Docker Compose Export** tab.\n2. Click **Download Production Compose Bundle**. The browser downloads a `.zip` or `.tar.gz` archive containing `docker-compose.yml`, `Caddyfile`, and `.env.example`.\n3. Extract the bundle on your production host:\n   ```bash\n   mkdir -p /opt/kanban-prod && cd /opt/kanban-prod\n   unzip ~/Downloads/kanban-compose-export.zip\n   ```\n4. Copy your standalone PostgreSQL data volume:\n   ```bash\n   docker run --rm -v kanban_data:/from:ro -v kanban_prod_pgdata:/to alpine cp -a /from/. /to/\n   ```\n5. Review `.env.example`, populate production domain names and TLS certificates, and rename to `.env`.\n6. Start the production stack:\n   ```bash\n   docker compose up -d\n   ```\n7. Verify container status and network compartmentalization:\n   ```bash\n   docker compose ps\n   docker compose exec app kanban-ctl health --full\n   ```\n\n![Standalone Status Studio - Mobile Portrait](images/slice4-standalone-390x844-mobile-portrait.png)\n"
